WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:05.930
You know the moon, right — the big space
rock humans landed on for the first time 50

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.129
years ago?

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:10.460
Well, astronauts left a lot of stuff behind
there.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:16.330
Stuff like used scientific equipment, clothing,
food wrappers and, well…

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.680
their poop.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.420
Across all 6 moon landings, Apollo astronauts
left 96 bags of human waste on the moon.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.689
We’re talking poop.

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:25.689
Pee.

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:26.689
Vomit.

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:29.810
It was left behind to save weight for the
return trip.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:34.040
Stuffed in big trash baggies, like this one,
dropped from Apollo 11

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.410
Inside those bags is a big, unanswered question
— a scientific mystery that I want answered:

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.269
Is there anything alive in that moon poop?

00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:46.060
I know, I know, it sounds ridiculous.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.780
But the answer might help us
understand how life started on Earth.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:56.560
And our potential to contaminate other worlds
— like Mars — with our literal shit.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.600
And the answer, it's not too far away.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:04.260
It’s lying in wait, nearly 240,000 miles away,
on the moon.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.300
Right here. Those — this really isn't to scale.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.860
What I’m saying is we gotta go back for
that shit.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.300
Human feces can be disgusting, sure. This is not — this is Play Doh.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.180
But it’s also teeming with life.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:27.230
Around half of its mass is made up of bacteria,
representing some of the 1,000-plus species

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.659
of microbes that live in our guts.

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:34.670
With the moon landings, we took microbial
life on Earth to the most extreme environment

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:35.670
it has ever been in.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.780
And we don’t know if it can survive there.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.850
In all the ways Earth is so hospitable to
life, the moon is not.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.250
It does not have a protective magnetic field
to deflect the most powerful and damaging

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:47.560
cosmic radiation.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.520
It doesn't have an ozone layer to absorb
the sun’s ultraviolet rays.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.090
It doesn't have an atmosphere.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.090
And the moon is subjected to wild temperature
swings over day and night:

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:01.920
A lot would have to go right for the microbes
to still be alive — or at least revivable

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.009
from a dormant state.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:05.390
But there are some scientists who want to go back and check,

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.180
because there are two really fascinating things that
we can learn from this poop.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.300
The first has to do with a planet millions
of miles away from the moon: Mars.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.170
For decades, scientists have wondered if there’s
life on Mars — and they’re getting closer

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.600
to figuring it out.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:28.080
Mars has a thin atmosphere, evidence of flowing
water, and a more hospitable environment than

00:02:28.091 --> 00:02:29.091
the moon.

00:02:29.091 --> 00:02:34.090
If microbes can survive on the moon, they’re
even more likely to survive on Mars.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:38.180
So what if humans go to Mars and leave their
poop there and the microbes in it start

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.180
to spread?

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.090
It will be hard to tell what life we find
there is Martian and what life we brought

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.380
with us if we accidentally contaminate the
planet with our poop.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.550
But there’s also a bigger question.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:52.830
There’s a hypothesis about how life started
on Earth: that it didn’t start on Earth

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:53.830
at all.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:59.500
That some asteroid or comet, carrying some
microbes, crashed-landed here and seeded life on the planet.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.680
Finding living things in the moon poop could
support that hypothesis, because if microbial

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:08.640
life can survive on the moon, it could survive
on an asteroid, too.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.440
And if the hypothesis is true, it could mean
life travels across the universe, jumping

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.260
from planet-to-planet, from asteroid collisions.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.060
Here’s a wilder thought: Let’s say an
asteroid comes hurtling by, slams into the

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:27.599
moon, and projects the Apollo mission moon poop
into the deep reaches of space.

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:30.050
Could that seed life in the broader universe?

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:31.050
Maybe.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:34.480
So how do we figure out the answer to these
great mysteries?

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.880
Easy.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:36.800
We go back.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:43.320
That's gonna take a lot of work and research and diving into internet clickholes.

